text = "Richmond.\nHoward and Another v. Landsberg's Committee.\n1. County Courts \u2014 Jurisdiction\u2014Judgments\u2014Collateral Attack. \u2014 The former county courts of this State were, in respect to purely judicial powers, courts of general jurisdiction, and their judgments are presumed to be right and cannot be collaterally assailed.\n2. Judgments \u2014 Collateral Attack.- \u2014 In an action of ejectment by the committee of a lunatic against a purchaser of the lunatic\u2019s land which was sold under a judicial proceeding against a former committee, objection cannot be made that the lunatic had no notice of the application for the appointment of his former committee, and the silence or incompleteness of the record of the court appointing the committee will be of no avail in a collateral attack on such appointment. Judgments of courts of. general jurisdiction, acting within the scope of their authority, are not open to collateral attack.\n3. Insane Persons \u2014 Adjudication\u2014Irregularity-\u2014Restoration\u2014Estoppel. If a lunatic who has had no notice of the appointment of his committee comes into court and asks that his committee be required to restore to him such of his property as he has in his hands, on the ground that he has been restored to sanity, instead of demanding to have the proceedings set aside by reason of their alleged irregularity he thereby solemnly admits of record that the proceedings against him were valid, and will not be thereafter permitted to impeach them to the prejudice of an innocent purchaser of his land, even though he did not appear until after the land had been sold.\n4. Deeds oe Commissioners \u2014 Recitals\u2014Presumption.\u2014If the recitals in a deed from a commissioner of a court are sufficient to show that the sale was regularly made in accordance with the decrees of the court directing it, then by express terms of section 3333a, Code of 1904, the deed is prima facie evidence that such sale was regularly made and that the other recitals of the deed are true, and it is unnecessary to introduce any portion of the record in support of such recitals until such presumption has been overcome.\n5. Insane Persons \u2014 Parties-\u2014Committees\u2014Guardian Ad Litem. \u2014 In a suit to subject the lands of a lunatic to the payment of his debts, the lunatic is not a necessary party, when he has a committee clothed with absolute power over him and his estate, together with authority to sue and be sued with respect to such estate. In a proceeding affecting the property rights of an insane person, it is the duty of the court, if he have no committee, to appoint a guardian ad Utem to represent and protect his interest, but if he has a committee the appointment of a guardian ad Utem is wholly unnecessary, except only where there is a conflict of interest between the committee and the lunatic.\nError to a judgment of the Corporation Court of the city of Hewport Hews, in an action of ejectment. Judgment for the plaintiff. Defendants assign error.\nReversed.\n8. Gordon Gumming, for the plaintiffs in error.\nAshby & Read, for the defendant in error.\nOn the 17th day of December, 1886, upon the application of his wife, the county court of Elizabeth City county appointed P. W. Phillips as committee of Julius Landsberg, who had theretofore been adjudged a lunatic and committed to the Eastern Lunatic Asylum, hut revoked the powers of Phillips as committee, by an order entered Eebruary 1, 1887, and committed the estate of the lunatic to Andrew Williams, sheriff of the county of Elizabeth City. Subsequent to the entry of this last mentioned order, a chancery suit was brought in the Circuit Court of Warwick county by Wm. S. Howard against Andrew Williams, sheriff, and as such committee of Julius Landsberg, praying a sale of a certain house and lot in Uewport Hews, the property of the lunatic, to satisfy a debt due by him to the plaintiff. To that suit Julius Landsherg was not, eo nomine, made a party.\nThe sale asked for was decreed, and made by Thomas Tahb, special commissioner of the court for the purpose, and the property bought by Wm. S. Howard, who received a deed to the lot from Tabh, commissioner, dated September 25, 1888; and Howard took possession of the property under the deed, and continued in possession thereof until his death in 1892, and thereafter up to a few months before the trial of this suit his widow and sole heir, plaintiffs in error, received through the hands of the officers of the court, appointed in a creditor\u2019s suit to settle the estate of said Wm. S. Howard, deceased, the rents accrued from the property; so that the property had been in the open, notorious and adverse possession of the decedent, Howard, his widow and sole heir at law, respectively, and the officers of the court, from September, 1888, and taxes thereon had been regularly paid by the Howards or their representatives to the time of the institution of this suit in September, 1905.\nJulius Landsherg was discharged from the asylum, as restored to sanity, in March, 1890, and remained at large, engaging in such employment as he saw fit to undertake, including the duties of a United States postmaster, until July, 1899, when he was again committed to the asylum.\nOn May 19, 1890, Landsherg appeared in the Oounty Court of Elizabeth City county, produced the certificate of the superintendent of the asylum that he was restored to sanity, and upon his motion an order was made that his committee surrender to him such estate as was then in the committee\u2019s hands; therefore, as plaintiffs in error contend, the statute of limitations then began to run in favor of Howard, the grantee in the deed from Tabb, commissioner, from March, 1890, and continued to run, notwithstanding the recurrence of Landsberg\u2019s insanity in 1899"
summary = generate(summary_model, summary_tokenizer, text, max_new_tokens=1028).replace("</s>", "").replace("<pad>", "").strip()
irac_template = """These are Legal Memorandum written for various cases, with thoughtful and convincing discussions with issues, rules, analysis and conclusions.

CASE 1:

Santiago Milian, also known as Santiago Jimenez, appealed from a summary denial of his post-conviction relief petition under Florida Rule of Criminal Procedure 3.850. He initially filed a timely motion raising nine claims. The trial court dismissed claims three through eight with prejudice and deemed claims one, two, and nine legally insufficient but allowed 21 days for amendments. This decision was non-final and non-appealable. Milian amended his motion as directed, but the trial court failed to issue a final, appealable order on all claims. Notably, the trial court should have allowed 60 days for amendment, not just 21, as per the rule. Upon review, the appellate court found that the trial court had only ruled on six claims and dismissed the remaining three without prejudice. Milian did amend these claims accordingly. Consequently, the appellate court reversed the initial order and remanded the case for further proceedings, emphasizing the need for the trial court to address the amended motion comprehensively.

LEGAL MEMORANDOUM 1 WITH ISSUE, RULE, ANALYSIS AND CONCLUSION:
Issue
Did the trial court err by denying Santiago Milian's motion for post-conviction relief without providing the required 60 days to amend his motion and subsequently failing to issue a final, appealable order addressing all claims?

Rule
Under Florida Rule of Criminal Procedure 3.850, a defendant who files a timely motion for post-conviction relief that raises multiple claims must be granted 60 days to amend any claims found to be legally insufficient but not dismissed with prejudice. This rule is designed to ensure that defendants have a fair opportunity to present their case fully. A final, appealable order must be issued by the trial court, disposing of all claims raised in the motion.

Analysis
Santiago Milian filed a Rule 3.850 motion raising nine separate claims for post-conviction relief. The trial court initially denied three of these claims and found three others to be legally insufficient, giving Milian only 21 days to amend these insufficient claims. This decision contradicts Rule 3.850, which clearly mandates a 60-day period for amendment of insufficient claims.
Furthermore, the trial court's failure to rule on the amended motion and issue a final, appealable order covering all claims leaves the defendant in a state of legal limbo, unable to seek further judicial review or relief. This oversight undermines the procedural safeguards intended by Rule 3.850, potentially impairing Milian's right to adequate and fair legal process.
The appellate court noted these procedural errors and rightfully reversed the trial court's decision, remanding the case for further proceedings. This decision is consistent with the appellate court's role in ensuring that lower courts adhere to established legal standards and procedural rules, which are crucial for maintaining the integrity of the judicial system and protecting the rights of individuals.

Conclusion
The trial court erred in its handling of Santiago Milian's motion for post-conviction relief by not allowing the mandated 60 days to amend his insufficient claims and by failing to issue a final, appealable order. The appellate court's decision to reverse and remand the case was appropriate and necessary to correct these procedural errors and ensure compliance with the rules governing post-conviction relief. Moving forward, the trial court should adhere strictly to the requirements of Florida Rule of Criminal Procedure 3.850 in reconsidering the defendant's amended motion and in all future cases involving similar procedural contexts.

CASE 2:

%(case)s

LEGAL MEMORANDOUM 2 WITH ISSUE, RULE, ANALYSIS AND CONCLUSION:
Issue
"""

